** Opener's reopening after an overcall and responder's pass

Responder's pass over a one-level overcall is often a trap pass with length in
their suit (a negative double would have shown the other suits), so opener,
short in their suit, reopens with a double; opener rebids a six-card suit;
otherwise opener passes. After the reopening double responder passes for
penalty with four cards in their suit, bids notrump with a stopper, and
otherwise returns to opener's suit, which carries no requirement of its own.
The partnership then stops.

#CUT reopen-answer
P = {prio:3} 6+ hcp, 4+\Y
  #STOP
3N = {prio:2} 13+ hcp
  #STOP
2N = {prio:2} 11--12 hcp
  #STOP
\R = {prio:-1} any hand
  #STOP
#ENDCUT

1X-(1Y)-P-(P)-
D = {prio:2} 12+ hcp, 0--2Y
2X = {prio:1} 12--16 hcp, 6+X
  #STOP
P = {prio:-100} {stop} any hand

1X-(1Y)-P-(P)-D-(P)-
1N = {prio:2} 6--10 hcp
  #STOP
#PASTE reopen-answer \Y=Y \R=2X

1X-(1Y)-P-(P)-D-(any)-
P = {prio:-100} {stop} any hand

1Y-(2X)-P-(P)-
D = {prio:2} 12+ hcp, 0--2X
2Y = {prio:1} 12--16 hcp, 6+Y
  #STOP
P = {prio:-100} {stop} any hand

1Y-(2X)-P-(P)-D-(P)-
#PASTE reopen-answer \Y=X \R=2Y

1Y-(2X)-P-(P)-D-(any)-
P = {prio:-100} {stop} any hand

1X-(2Y)-P-(P)-
D = {prio:2} 12+ hcp, 0--2Y
3X = {prio:1} 12--16 hcp, 6+X
  #STOP
P = {prio:-100} {stop} any hand

1X-(2Y)-P-(P)-D-(P)-
#PASTE reopen-answer \Y=Y \R=3X

1X-(2Y)-P-(P)-D-(any)-
P = {prio:-100} {stop} any hand

** Opener after they compete over our stop (NOTES.md #P10)

The system stops let the partnership pass with any hand once one of us has passed,
whatever the opponents do. At the positions below that answer is not SAYC's, so
they get real rows; the stop pass stays as the lowest-ranked call for the rest.

*** After the negative double of a two-level overcall and their raise

Opener is no longer forced to bid. With four cards in the major the double
promised he competes (game with 17+); without a major to show over their raise of
a major, the double showed the minors and opener bids a four-card minor at the
four level with 15+; he rebids a six-card suit with 15+ (game in a major with
18+). Otherwise he passes.

After a minor opening (M is the doubler's unbid major, `cM` its cheapest level:
3H/3S, or 4H over their 3S where only game remains):

1m-(2Y)-D-(3Y)-
4M = {prio:3} 17+ hcp, 4+M
  #STOP
cM = {prio:1} 4+M
  #STOP
cm = {prio:0} 15+ hcp, 6+m
  #STOP
P = {prio:-100} {stop} any hand

After a major opening and their minor overcall:

1M-(2m)-D-(3m)-
4oM = {prio:3} 17+ hcp, 4+oM
  #STOP
3oM = {prio:1} 4+oM
  #STOP
4M = {prio:2} 18+ hcp, 6+M
  #STOP
3M = {prio:0} 15+ hcp, 6+M
  #STOP
P = {prio:-100} {stop} any hand

After a major opening and their overcall in the other major the double showed
the minors:

1H-(2S)-D-(3S)-
4C = {prio:2} 15+ hcp, 4+!c
  #STOP
4D = {prio:2} 15+ hcp, 4+!d
  #STOP
4H = {prio:0} 15+ hcp, 6+!h
  #STOP
P = {prio:-100} {stop} any hand

1S-(2H)-D-(3H)-
4C = {prio:2} 15+ hcp, 4+!c
  #STOP
4D = {prio:2} 15+ hcp, 4+!d
  #STOP
4S = {prio:2} 18+ hcp, 6+!s
  #STOP
3S = {prio:0} 15+ hcp, 6+!s
  #STOP
P = {prio:-100} {stop} any hand

*** Opener's reopening over a three-level overcall

Responder's pass over a three-level overcall may be a penalty pass, so opener,
short in their suit, reopens with a takeout double (12+); he rebids a six-card
suit with 15+. Responder then passes for penalty with four cards in their suit,
bids 3NT with 9+ and a stopper, and otherwise returns to opener's suit.

1Y-(3X)-P-(P)-
D = {prio:2} 12+ hcp, 0--1X
3Y = {prio:1} 15+ hcp, 6+Y
  #STOP
P = {prio:-100} {stop} any hand

1Y-(3X)-P-(P)-D-(P)-
P = {prio:3} 6+ hcp, 4+X
  #STOP
3N = {prio:2} 9+ hcp, stopper, 0--3X
  #STOP
3Y = {prio:-1} any hand
  #STOP

1Y-(3X)-P-(P)-D-(any)-
P = {prio:-100} {stop} any hand

1X-(3Y)-P-(P)-
D = {prio:2} 12+ hcp, 0--1Y
4X = {prio:1} 15+ hcp, 6+X
  #STOP
P = {prio:-100} {stop} any hand

1X-(3Y)-P-(P)-D-(P)-
P = {prio:3} 6+ hcp, 4+Y
  #STOP
3N = {prio:2} 9+ hcp, stopper, 0--3Y
  #STOP
4X = {prio:-1} any hand
  #STOP

1X-(3Y)-P-(P)-D-(any)-
P = {prio:-100} {stop} any hand

*** Opener after their takeout double, responder's pass and advancer's 1NT

Advancer's 1NT is natural (6--10); opener doubles it for penalty with 16+ and
rebids a six-card suit with less.

1X-(D)-P-(1N)-
D = {prio:2} 16+ hcp
  #STOP
2X = {prio:1} 12--15 hcp, 6+X
  #STOP
P = {prio:-100} {stop} any hand

*** Opener after responder's pass and their raise of a one-level overcall

Responder's pass denied a negative double, but opener, short in their suit, may
still reopen with a takeout double (13+); he rebids a six-card suit with 15+.
After the double responder bids an unbid major with four cards at the cheapest
level (a minor when both majors are gone), and otherwise returns to opener's
suit, which carries no requirement.

The six opener/overcall pairs share one table: X is opener's suit, Y the
one-level overcall above it (1C-(1D) up to 1H-(1S)). Responder's cheapest unbid
major is `cM` (2H/2S over their raise of a minor, 3H over their raise of
spades); when both majors are gone (1H-(1S)) he bids a four-card minor instead.
Before phase 4's relative levels this was six literal tables (NOTES.md #P12,
"P10 refactor").

1X-(1Y)-P-(2Y)-
D = {prio:2} 13+ hcp, 0--1Y
3X = {prio:1} 15+ hcp, 6+X
  #STOP
P = {prio:-100} {stop} any hand

1X-(1Y)-P-(2Y)-D-(P)-
cM = {prio:2} 4+M
  #STOP
3X = {prio:-1} any hand
  #STOP

1H-(1S)-P-(2S)-D-(P)-
3C = {prio:2} 4+!c
  #STOP
3D = {prio:2} 4+!d
  #STOP

1X-(1Y)-P-(2Y)-D-(any)-
P = {prio:-100} {stop} any hand

*** Responder after 1NT-3NT when a passed hand bids on

Responder's 10--15 opposite 15--17 is the balance of power: he doubles a
balancing four-level suit bid for penalty with 10+.

1N-(P)-3N-(P)-P-(4X)-
D = {prio:1} 10+ hcp
  #STOP
P = {prio:-100} {stop} any hand
